WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.560
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.120
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.800
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.760
In other words,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.720
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.600
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.840
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.440
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.240
Today we have the first
in a two part series

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.320
where I offer some of the highlights
from my latest book, How Not to Age,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.040
taped in front of a live audience.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:37.160
There may be no such thing
as dying from old age.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.880
From a study of more than
42,000 consecutive autopsies,

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.840
centenarians—
those who live to be a hundred—

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.880
were found to have
succumbed-to-disease

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.320
in a hundred percent
of the cases.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.000
Though most were perceived,
even by their physicians,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.920
to have been healthy
just prior to death,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.000
not one,
“died, of old age.”

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.840
They died from disease,
most commonly, heart disease.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.320
If aging kills via diseases,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.080
why wasn’t my book
How Not to Die

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.400
all the longevity book
anyone needs?

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.880
In it, I ran through
preventing, arresting,

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.160
or reversing each
of our top 15 killers—

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.440
starting with
heart disease,

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.760
not only the #1 killer
of centenarians,

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.880
but of men and women
across the board

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.520
and projected to remain that
way in the decades to come.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.680
But is it really?

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.880
Because the single
greatest risk factor

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.800
for most of our
killer diseases

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.960
is how old you are,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.080
one could argue that the
leading cause of death

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.520
is actually aging.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.080
The rate of death
increases exponentially

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.800
for age-related
diseases,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.920
such as heart disease,
cancer, stroke, dementia.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.520
So yes, within the
same age bracket,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.240
having a high cholesterol

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.080
can increase your risk of
heart disease up to 20-fold,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.320
but an 80-year-old may have
five hundred times the risk

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.240
of having a heart attack

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.000
compared to a
twenty-year-old.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.280
Now, the reason we focus
on things like cholesterol

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.680
is because it’s a
modifiable risk factor,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:32.160
but what if the rate of
aging was modifiable too?

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.880
Instead of our current
piecemeal approach

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.440
of focusing on
individual diseases,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.880
what about slowing down
the aging process itself?

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.880
When I was a
nerdy little kid,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.080
I wanted to cure
cancer when I grew up.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.040
But even if all forms
of cancer were eliminated,

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.000
the average life
expectancy in the U.S.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.320
would only go up
about three years.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:02:59.820
Why?

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.640
Because dodging cancer
would just mean delaying death

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.320
from a heart attack or stroke.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:11.320
If one age-related ailment
doesn’t get us, another will.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.240
So, rather than playing
“whack-a-mole”

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.920
by tackling each
disease separately,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.960
slowing the
rate of aging

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.720
could potentially address
all these issues simultaneously.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.040
Imagine if there
were an intervention

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.560
that didn’t just reduce your
risk of the leading killers,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.720
but also arthritis,
osteoporosis,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.360
sensory impairments.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.840
Because risks tend to
double every seven years—

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.240
like if the average 65-year-old
had the health of a 58-year-old?

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.680
Slowing aging by
even just seven years

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.240
could cut everyone’s
risk of death, frailty,

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.400
and disability in half.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.680
That’s why I wrote
How Not to Age.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.720
The problem is that
the anti-aging field

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.080
is said to be a “fertile
ground for cons,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.920
scams, and get-rich-
quick schemes.”

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.320
As a former president of the
Gerontological Society wrote,

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.000
there have been “few subjects
which have been more misleading

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.880
to the uncritical and more
proﬁtable to the unscrupulous.”

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.440
Not only does the popular
literature on the subject

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.600
harbor a “huge amount
of misinformation,”

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.560
but most age-researching
scientists widely known to the public

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.680
are said to be “unscrupulous
purveyors of useless nostrums,”

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.080
according to the Editor-in-Chief
of a leading gerontology journal.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.200
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.320
as to what to feed
ourselves and our families,

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:49.360
we should rely not on
anecdote, but on evidence.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.680
That’s why I cite
everything to the teeth.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.000
How Not to Die,
had about 2,000 citations.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.840
How Not to Diet,
5,000 citations.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:08.680
How Not to Age ended
up with 13,000 citations,

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.320
all of which I have
hyperlinked for you online

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.560
to access all the
original sources.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.760
So, you can
download the studies

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:26.000
and read them
all yourself.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.760
My aim was to cover
every possible angle

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.320
for developing the
optimal diet and lifestyle,

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.240
for the longest,
healthiest lifespan—

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.280
based on the best available
balance of evidence.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.440
My inspiration for writing the book
was a consensus document,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.200
“Interventions to Slow
Aging in Humans”,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.560
compiled by the top researchers
in the anti-aging field

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.520
(the likes of Drs. Longo,
Sinclair, Fontana).

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.400
They were
brought together

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.240
to identify the most
promising strategies

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.240
to combat aging.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.000
They identified a list
of “essential pathways,”

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.480
for example, drugs that
can block the hormone IGF-1,

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.920
or drugs to block the
pro-aging enzyme TOR.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.720
But I realized as
I looked at this:

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.320
Wait, every single
one of these pathways

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:21.360
could be regulated
through diet.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.280
That became the first
section of the book.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.840
The term “antiaging”

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.800
has been much
abused in popular culture,

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.440
attached to all manner
of unproven products

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.120
and procedures,

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.560
whereas the term
should really be reserved

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.680
for things that can
delay or reverse aging

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.520
by targeting one
of the established

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.720
aging mechanisms,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.040
the so-called
Hallmarks of Aging,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.160
the common denominators
of the aging process like,

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.280
for example, the buildup
of misfolded proteins

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.680
that can be disposed of
through autophagy.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.400
See, at any given time,

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.160
most of our cells
are producing

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.120
and assembling more
than 10,000 distinct proteins,

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.400
each of which can
become misfolded

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.280
or damaged
at any time

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.200
and require a
cleanup in aisle three.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.960
But during times
of nutrient excess,

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.880
our body figures
why bother?

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.440
We can just toss it in
the corner and make another.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.320
Having evolved in
a context of scarcity,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.880
our body expects to fall
on hard times any day now

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.160
and can put off spring
cleaning until then.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.560
But these days,

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.400
these lean times
hardly ever come;

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:40.240
so, our cells just end up
continually hoarding junk.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.240
That’s where autophagy
comes from,

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.600
from the Greek words
for “self-eating.”

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.160
It’s a housekeeping
process

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:53.000
by which defective cellular
components are broken down

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.560
and scrapped
for spare parts.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.640
This doubles as both salvage
operation and quality control,

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.240
clearing out some
of the damaged debris

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.880
implicated in the
aging process,

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:08.120
while renewing our cells
in a sort of cellular reset.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:15.200
As one review put it,
“The janitor is the undercover boss.”

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.400
Our ancient ancestors often
went for several days without food;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.920
so, autophagy was
constantly being switched on.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:22.960
But these days,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.880
our cells no longer need
to clean out the corners

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.160
for sustenance.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.600
And so, the tainted heaps
just pile higher and higher,

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:31.440
which isn’t good,

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.000
because autophagy is
essential for lifespan extension.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.720
It’s not only necessary,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.880
but sometimes sufficient
for increasing longevity.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:45.880
Self-digestion for
lifespan extension.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.080
Boosting autophagy
alone can boost lifespan,

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.160
at least in mice,

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.160
by an average of 12 percent,
and it also boosts healthspan.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.240
Unfortunately, our bodies’
ability to take-out-the-trash

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.800
declines with age,
leading to this vicious cycle.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.280
Garbage builds up,
accelerating aging,

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.200
which leads to more
garbage buildup.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.640
No wonder the drug
industry is so eager

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.880
to pharmacologically modulate
autophagy to combat aging—

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:20.120
but we can do it naturally.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.960
One way is
through fasting.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:22.600
Unfortunately,

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.640
autophagy doesn’t
really maximally ramp up

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.400
until after a day
or two of fasting,

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.560
which may be too long
to do it unsupervised.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.040
Fasting more than 24 hours

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.240
should probably only be done
under medical supervision,

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.360
ideally in a
live-in clinic.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.200
In other words,
don’t try this at home.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.920
This is not just legalistic
mumbo-jumbo.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:43.840
For example,
normally,

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.600
your kidneys dive into
sodium conservation mode,

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:50.680
but should that
response break down,

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.800
you could rapidly develop
an electrolyte abnormality

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.760
that might only manifest
with non-specific symptoms

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.320
like fatigue or dizziness,

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.800
which could be easily
dismissed, until it’s too late.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:03.880
Thankfully,

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.200
there’s another way
to activate autophagy.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.400
You can fast or go fast.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.560
Exercise induces
autophagy.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:13.760
As one of the
researchers said,

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:16.480
“I’ve always known
exercise was good for you,

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.440
but when we found that
it increases autophagy,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:23.320
I finally got a treadmill.”

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.040
You have to do
enough, though.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:28.160
Autophagy is activated
after 60 minutes

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.120
of moderate
intensity exercise,

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:35.000
but only twenty minutes
fails to move the needle.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.600
What about diet?
Any foods to avoid?

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.920
In 2021, we found
out acrylamide

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.760
can inhibit autophagy,

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.560
at least in cells
in a petri dish.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.360
Acrylamide is a toxin

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:52.200
concentrated in French
fries and potato chips

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.760
that’s formed during
the frying process.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:57.400
The fact that high acrylamide
exposure is associated

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:00.720
with as much as double
the risk of premature death

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.520
would be consistent with
an anti-autophagy effect,

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:09.400
though diminished lifespans
among eaters of fatty, salty snacks

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.840
isn’t exactly a revelation.

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:14.760
You can’t know if
it's cause and effect,

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:21.920
until you… put it to the test—
very nice!

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.480
Before being asked to
eat a bag of potato chips

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.040
every day for a month,

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.960
study subjects were given
weeks of boiled potatoes

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.640
mixed with the same amount
of fat and salt as the chips.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.920
Compared with the fatty
salty boiled potatoes,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:40.640
their C-reactive protein levels
shot up 50 percent on the chips,

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.840
suggesting “chronic ingestion
of acrylamide-containing products

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.360
induces a proinﬂammatory state.”

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.240
However, deep frying
causes the formation

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:54.800
of all manner of
“undesired food-borne toxicants;”

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:58.400
so, we can’t be sure
it was the acrylamide.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.480
As one of the earliest
geriatric medicine textbooks

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:06.920
presciently concluded
back in 1849,

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:14.040
“frying…is an abomination.”

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:17.800
If you just must
have your French fry fix,

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:25.080
air-frying produces about
90 percent less acrylamide.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.280
Anything that can help?

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:30.480
Starving yourself
“generates discomfort,”

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.200
but there is something
that activates autophagy

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.880
that many people
find comforting:

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:42.600
coffee.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:44.160
At a human-
equivalent dose,

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:48.240
both regular and decaf rapidly
induce autophagy within hours—

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:48.960
in mice,

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.200
and coffee can extend
the lifespans of some rats,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:53.440
but what about people?

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.120
In humans, we only
have observational research,

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:57.600
but to date
about 20 studies

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.440
have followed more than
10 million people over time,

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:03.120
and those drinking
3 cups (720 ml) of coffee a day

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.880
had 13 percent lower risk

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:10.000
of dying from all
causes put together.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.680
And decaf appears
to be just as protective;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:16.480
so, it’s not the caffeine.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:20.600
Coffee contains more than
a thousand bioactive compounds.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.600
The polyphenol chlorogenic acid

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:25.800
is the most abundant
antioxidant in coffee;

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:28.320
so, researchers started there,
and indeed,

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:33.600
was found to enhance
autophagy in human cells.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.960
More than a hundred
coffees have been tested,

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:41.000
and chlorogenic acid levels
vary by more than 30-fold.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:46.760
Interestingly, the major contributor
to this wide range is Starbucks,

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:50.680
with its extremely low
chlorogenic acid content,

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:55.680
thought to be because they
roast their beans so dark

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.760
that they destroy it.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:03.600
Freeze-drying is okay,

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:09.080
and brewed has
more than espresso.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.400
Paper filtered
is the best,

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.920
because it traps the cholesterol-
raising compounds in coffee,

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:18.440
perhaps explaining why
those drinking filtered coffee

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:24.760
had even lower mortality rates
than those drinking unfiltered coffee.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:28.720
Any food components
that activate autophagy?

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:29.360
Spermidine,

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:34.440
the longevity elixir,
spermidine.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:42.920
Don’t be put off
by the name.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:46.160
Spermidine and its
byproduct spermine

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:48.520
are actually found
throughout the body.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:52.120
When it was later independently
discovered in the brain and the muscles,

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:55.200
they called it
neuridine and musculamine

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.680
until they found out it was
all the same compound,

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:02.720
and so, naming rights defaulted
to the less palatable original.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:05.480
Our body can
make it from scratch,

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:09.320
but we can also boost levels
by eating spermidine-rich foods,

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:11.440
which is good news

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.120
because our spermidine
levels tend to decline with age,

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.560
dropping more than half
by the time we reach our 50s.

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:22.160
This decline is seen across
the biological spectrum

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.760
with one
remarkable exception,

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.320
naked mole rats,

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.960
also known by their
more cuddly nickname:

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.040
sand puppy,

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:37.840
considered to be a
“non-aging mammal”

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.940
without any visible
signs of aging

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:44.680
and almost no decline
in physiological function over decades,

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:47.200
or typical signs of aging,
like loss of fertility or muscle mass.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:49.840
Perhaps in part

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:55.480
because they are able to maintain
their high levels of spermidine,

00:15:55.480 --> 00:16:00.680
something you also see
in human centenarians.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.600
To prove cause and effect,
extra spermidine was fed to animals

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:08.040
and “Induction of Autophagy by
Spermidine Promotes Longevity,”

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:12.800
increasing the lifespans of mice,
for example, by as much as 25 percent.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:17.600
In a database of more than
a thousand life-extending compounds,

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:20.120
among the small subset
with the fewest side effects,

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:25.560
spermidine has the largest
documented lifespan extension.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.160
Longevity can be improved
even when started late in life,

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.160
kind of the human equivalent
of changing your diet

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:32.840
when you’re already
in your fifties.

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:36.040
Anti-aging effects were
found in the heart and kidneys,

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:39.640
rejuvenating immune function,
delaying brain aging,

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:41.800
and improving
cognitive function.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:45.680
Yeah, but that was in animals
like fruit flies and mice.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:51.760
I mean who cares if spermidine
cures flies of senior moments.

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:55.120
What about in people?

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.960
Hundreds of men and women
in their 40s through 80s

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:57.960
were followed for twenty years,

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:01.680
and after looking at 146
different components of their diets,

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:06.480
the single most predictive
of longevity was spermidine.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:07.720
How much spermidine?

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:12.480
Higher spermidine intake
is linked to lower mortality.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:14.600
Those who consumed
the most spermidine

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:17.360
had a reduced risk of death
from all major causes,

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:20.440
which is what we’d expect
from an anti-aging compound.

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:23.040
Critically, this survival
advantage persisted

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.280
even after controlling
for dietary excellence,

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:26.280
meaning that it
wasn’t just because

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.000
they were eating
healthier foods in general,

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:32.400
but rather spermidine-
rich foods in particular.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.120
How big of an effect
are we talking?

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:36.240
The reduction
in mortality risk

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:38.880
between getting more than
12 mg of spermidine a day

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.840
compared to
getting less than 9

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:42.960
was as if those eating
more spermidine

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.880
were 5.7 years younger.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.400
It’s as if by eating
more of certain foods,

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:53.440
they were effectively able
to turn back the clock nearly 6 years.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:55.080
The findings were
so extraordinary,

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.800
the researchers sought
to replicate their results

00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:59.960
in a whole new
set of individuals

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:06.400
and, indeed, arrived at
the same conclusion.

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:08.840
This led some to propose

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:13.120
that spermidine may
be an anti-aging vitamin.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.400
When we’re younger,

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.680
we seem to be able
to make enough,

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:19.080
but as we get older,

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:24.800
we may need to start ensuring
we’re getting enough in our diet

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.320
to maintain autophagy
into old age.

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:29.800
Well, if spermidine is going to be
considered an anti-aging vitamin,

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:32.280
where is that
vitamin found?

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:36.160
Beans are said to have
the highest natural amounts,

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:39.800
but I compiled a list of the
top spermidine sources,

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:45.373
and pig pancreas beat out
bean burritos for the bronze.

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:50.360
As a certified dark
green leafy snob,

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:54.840
I was begrudgingly impressed
to see lettuce score so high,

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:56.000
though lettuce is so light,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.960
a 100-gram serving
would be about 3 cups,

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:03.680
but even the spermidine in
little side salads could really add up.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:06.480
In the book, I spend a lot
of time going through the list,

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:11.200
but the single most concentrated
source is wheat germ,

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.240
with 2.5 mg of spermidine

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:16.160
in just the 7 grams
in a tablespoon.

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:18.120
It’s also the
cheapest source,

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.040
costing as little as
two cents per milligram.

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:27.320
You can’t get a lot
of pancreas for two cents.

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:30.960
Does wheat germ
actually do anything?

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:35.360
Let’s randomize people to
some dinner rolls and find out.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:37.000
A randomized,
double-blind pilot

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:37.880
in which older individuals

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.000
were secretly slipped
some spermidine

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.680
in the form of wheat germ
baked into bread rolls

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.120
vs placebo rolls with
wheat bran instead.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:50.120
And, those with mild dementia
improved way beyond

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:54.160
all available antidementia
drug treatments so far.

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:56.280
Admittedly, that’s
not saying much,

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:00.840
but what’s the harm of sprinkling
a little wheat germ on your food?

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:06.720
The latest Alzheimer's drugs
don’t appear to work at all.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:12.800
All you get for
your 56,000 dollars

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:20.760
is a dramatically increased risk
of swelling or bleeding into your brain.

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:24.200
When the FDA
approved it anyway,

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:28.440
the head of the
American Geriatrics Society replied,

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:33.360
“My head just exploded.”

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:37.560
(Maybe they just got
slipped a dose of the drug).

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.600
Anyway, lots of other
clinical spermidine studies,

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:41.120
but just to wrap
up this section,

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:45.440
autophagy is considered the
“primary system for cleaning the body”

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.480
from the inside out.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:51.040
And, we can boost autophagy
with aerobic exercise,

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:54.440
skipping fries and chips,
drinking coffee,

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:55.920
and eating specific foods

00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:01.080
to reach a target of
20 mg of spermidine a day.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.240
I offer similar diet
and lifestyle takeaways

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.400
for each of the other
10 anti-aging pathways,

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:08.280
but don’t have time
to touch on them,

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:13.480
because there’s still three
other major sections of the book.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:16.880
Part Two focuses on
the diets and lifestyles

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:21.720
of the healthiest and longest-lived
populations around the world.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:25.320
The odds of living to a hundred have
risen from approximately 1 in 20 million

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.000
to as high as 1 in 50.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.840
Why do some make it
and others not?

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:34.240
It’s not just a matter of
picking better parents.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:39.760
Studies following identical twins
suggest that only about 25 percent

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:43.400
of the variation in lifespan
is explained by genetics.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:45.440
So, what can we
do for the majority

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:48.560
over which we may
have some control?

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:52.200
The media loves stories
of hard-living centenarians

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:55.880
who attribute their longevity to
some combination of lard, vodka,

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:58.040
and favorite brand of cigarette,

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:05.560
but how do the oldest and
healthiest really eat and live?

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:08.680
That’s what we have
the blue zones for,

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:13.440
areas of exceptional
longevity around the world,

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:16.680
where there may
be ten times the rate

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:20.400
of those reaching
those triple digits.

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:24.440
(Named for the color a demographer
used in a global “heat map”

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.440
of mortality around the world.)

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:30.360
What lessons can we learn?

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.700
The Blue Zones organization
distilled findings

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.840
from more than
150 dietary surveys

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.480
from the world’s
longest-living people

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.720
to create a set of
ten food guidelines.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:42.680
The foundation of the
Blue Zones Food Guidelines,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:46.760
is making your diet at least
95 percent plant-based,

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:48.720
avoiding highly
processed foods,

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:51.520
emphasizing beans as the
healthiest source of protein,

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:55.640
water as the best beverage,
and nuts as the healthiest snack.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:58.040
That’s the foundation.

00:22:58.040 --> 00:23:00.480
The final five guidelines
are to “Go easy on fish,”

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.360
“Eliminate eggs,
“Slash sugar,”

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:05.640
“Reduce dairy,”
and “Retreat from meat,”

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:07.320
noting that blue zones
centenarians

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:08.880
only eat about
two ounces (57 g) of meat

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:12.040
or less about five
times a month.

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:16.320
Historically, there’ve been
five recognized blue zones,

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:20.800
but only one survives
and thrives to this day,

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:23.440
the red, white, and blue zone:

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:27.280
the Seventh-Day Adventists
in Loma Linda, California,

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:29.600
with perhaps the
longest life expectancy

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:34.680
of any formally studied
population in history.

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:38.240
There are a number of shared
Blue Zone lifestyle characteristics:

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.200
family coherence,
avoiding smoking,

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:42.320
daily exercise,
social engagement,

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:48.160
but plant-based nutrition appears
to be the principal component,

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:51.920
alone accounting for about
half the difference in lifespan.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:55.800
No surprise, since the number
one risk factor for death

00:23:55.800 --> 00:24:00.960
in the United States
is the American diet.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:09.040
So, unsafe sex is bad.
Sedentary lifestyle? Bad.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:13.640
Alcohol and drugs—
especially tobacco. Bad.

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:17.240
But cigarettes only kill about
a half million Americans every year,

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.760
whereas our diet
kills many more.

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:22.000
We are what we eat.

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.600
Which is good news,

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:27.800
because it means
we have the power.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:32.080
Changing from a more typical
diet to a more optimized diet

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:34.000
starting at age twenty
would be expected

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:35.360
to increase the
lifespan of women

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:39.120
by about eleven years
and men by thirteen years.

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:42.280
The largest life
expectancy gain

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:45.659
would be made by
eating more legumes—

00:24:45.659 --> 00:24:48.560
in other words, more beans,
split peas, chickpeas, and lentils.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:51.440
So, if there was one
thing we could eat:

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:57.760
Legumes for longevity.
Hummus for health!

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:02.320
Then, comes whole
grains and nuts,

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:06.040
and eating less meat.

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:10.200
Now, for the few of you
who aren’t twenty anymore,

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.080
not to worry.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.600
Starting to eat healthier
at age sixty,

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:18.920
could still mean adding eight or
nine more years to your lifespan.

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:23.080
Even starting as late as
age eighty could add years.

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:25.760
Changing your
health destiny

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:29.960
can start tomorrow
morning at breakfast.

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:32.000
And it doesn’t take much.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.520
The NIH-AARP study
is the largest

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:38.000
forward-looking study
on diet and health in history.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:42.600
Based on its six million
person-years of observation,

00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:47.320
replacing just three percent
of daily caloric intake

00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:50.520
from animal protein
with plant protein

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:53.640
was associated with a
ten percent decreased risk

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.520
of overall mortality
in both men and women.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:56.920
Just swapping three percent!

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:03.000
Of all the animal protein sources,
eggs were found to be the worst.

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.960
Swapping in three percent
of plant protein for egg protein

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.960
was associated with
twice the benefit,

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:13.200
twenty percent lower mortality
for swapping in a few British beans

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:16.320
for breakfast instead.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:19.920
Harvard researchers found that
when it comes to premature death,

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:22.600
processed meat was the worst,
followed by eggs.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.480
In essence, they found
that tuna salad

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.400
may be better than
egg salad or a BLT,

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:29.240
but a bean burrito
beat out the bunch.

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:32.640
When it came to death
from all causes put together,

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:35.480
plant protein beat out
every type of animal protein—

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:38.360
including dairy,
fish, and chicken.

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:41.080
A three percent swap
from chicken to chickpeas,

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:42.680
or fish to falafel

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:49.000
was linked to a six percent
decreased risk of premature death.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:54.840
But does eating healthy
actually slow down aging?

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:56.400
Randomize
hundreds of women

00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:00.320
to a diet centered around
healthy plant foods,

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:03.320
or exercise, or neither,

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.600
and though the physical
activity failed,

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:10.240
those in the plant-based
dietary intervention group

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:15.200
had a significant slowing
of biological aging.

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:18.080
Of course, it isn’t just about
adding years to your life,

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:19.800
but life to your years.

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:23.200
An unhealthy aging
index was devised

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:24.760
to measure functional
impairments,

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.520
vitality, mental health,
physical health.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:34.040
Substituting even just
one percent of calories

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:36.480
from plant protein
for animal protein

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:39.840
appeared to lead to significantly
less deficit accumulation.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:43.480
And substituting five percent
may reduce the risk of dying

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:47.840
from the greatest deficit,
dementia.

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:51.920
That may help explain why those
who don’t eat any meat at all

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:56.040
may be up to three times
less likely to become demented.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:57.880
But again,
it’s not all-or-nothing.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:00.280
In fact, the worst thing
about humanity’s diet

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:05.120
is neither animal nor
vegetable, but mineral:

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:06.320
sodium.

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:09.600
Here are the top five
fatal flaws of our diet.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.280
Millions of deaths may
be attributable every year

00:28:12.280 --> 00:28:14.160
to not getting enough
whole grains,

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:17.080
not eating enough fruit,
not enough nuts and seeds,

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:17.960
not enough vegetables.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.960
But the single deadliest
ingredient in humanity’s diet

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.360
is something we
get too much of:

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:25.240
and that’s salt,

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:28.720
our number one dietary
risk factor for death.

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:30.380
A recent whopping study,
for example,

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:31.800
of more than a
half million people

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:36.680
found that those who salted
their food at the table at age fifty

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:40.800
appeared to have about a
two-year lower life expectancy

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:43.240
compared to those
that didn’t.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:46.200
So, just swapping
out the salt shaker

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:47.440
for some salt-
free seasoning

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:50.760
or salt substitutes could
potentially add years to your life.

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:53.480
How do we know
it’s cause and effect?

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:56.560
Five kitchens at a
veterans’ retirement home

00:28:56.560 --> 00:29:01.040
were randomized into
two groups for a few years,

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:04.400
offering meals salted either
with regular salt or,

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.040
unbeknownst to them,

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:09.280
a 50/50 blend of regular salt,
sodium chloride,

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:13.720
with a salt substitute like these,
potassium chloride.

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:16.400
The kind of salt was the only
difference between the meals,

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:21.640
and cardiovascular disease death
rates plummeted by 40 percent

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:25.120
in the folks getting the
reduced sodium blend.

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:26.920
The new difference
in life expectancy

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:28.520
between the two
groups at age seventy

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:30.360
was equivalent
to that which

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:36.000
would have occurred
naturally in 14 years,

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:39.260
meaning simply switching
to even half potassium salt—

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:43.000
for which you wouldn’t even
be able to taste the difference of—

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:46.040
appeared to effectively make
people more than a decade younger

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:50.240
when it came to the
risk of premature death.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:53.080
What do the likes
of the Salt Institute

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:55.800
have to say about public
health recommendations

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.320
to reduce sodium intake?

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.440
In testimony before a
Congressional committee,

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:02.560
the presumption
that healthier diets

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:06.040
would cut healthcare
costs was challenged.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:10.480
“Indeed,” one processed
food defender testified,

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:15.440
“healthcare expenditures increase
if the lifespan is prolonged.”

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:19.280
If people eat healthier
and live longer,

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:22.520
it could be
more expensive,

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.720
noting that if tobacco
were banned,

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.440
the increase in
expected lifespan would,

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:35.520
“increase the cost of
care of old people.”

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.520
Think how expensive
it would be

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:39.400
if people started taking
care of themselves

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:45.480
and didn’t, conveniently,
die on time.

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:47.520
Ultra-processed foods,

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:50.200
often packed with added
salt, sugar, and fat,

00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:57.880
consistently account for more than
50 percent of our dietary caloric intake.

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:02.760
More than half of
our diet is junk.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.320
Not surprisingly,

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:09.840
those foods are associated with
a greater risk of dying prematurely.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:15.240
So, just cutting back on
animal foods isn’t enough.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:17.120
Healthy plant-based
diets are associated

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:18.920
with a significantly
lower risk of dying,

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:22.880
but unhealthy plant-
based diets are not.

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:24.440
In the Harvard cohorts,

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:27.240
the more you minimize
meat, eggs, and dairy,

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:29.960
the lower your
risk of death falls,

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:33.800
but that’s only if you’re
eating healthy plant foods.

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:35.800
If you, instead,
just pile on the junk,

00:31:35.800 --> 00:31:36.800
like chips and soda,

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:40.280
you can increase your
risk of death overall—

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:44.800
even if animal product
consumption remains low.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:47.880
Same in the Million
Veteran Program study:

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:51.600
Healthy plant foods,
reduced their risk of death.

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:57.160
But if your idea of a plant-
based diet is fries and a Coke,

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:01.280
you’re not doing your
body any favors.

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:03.280
Same with the risk
of cognitive impairment.

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:08.360
Only healthy plant
foods reduce risk.

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:11.200
The same with dementia
and depression.

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.120
The same with frailty.

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:19.080
Healthy plant foods good;
plant-based junk bad.

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:23.120
That’s why Cornell Professor
Emeritus of Biochemistry,

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:23.960
T. Colin Campbell,

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:27.840
coined the term
whole food, plant-based diet.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.280
As a physician,

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:32.280
terms like vegetarian or vegan
just tell me what you don’t eat.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:35.400
I mean, do you actually
eat vegetables?

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:40.040
Professor Campbell’s physician
son and daughter-in-law

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:43.440
tried putting a group of
vegetarians and vegans

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.720
on a whole food,
plant-based diet.

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:47.760
In eight weeks,
they lost ten pounds,

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:49.680
dropped their LDL
cholesterol 15 points.

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:51.080
In other words,

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:57.040
vegans may benefit from eating
a little more plant-based, too.

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:01.120
Now, meat is a problem.

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:02.280
In terms of lifespan,

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:05.400
eating a burger may
cut one’s life as short

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:07.920
as smoking two cigarettes.

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:09.960
So, if it wouldn’t
even occur to us

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:12.480
to light up before-
and-after lunch,

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:17.040
maybe we should choose
the bean burrito instead.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:20.000
There are certainly ways
to make meat safer, though.

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:20.480
For example,

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:23.200
one of the ways whole food,
plant-based diets can help

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:27.040
is by reducing the
load of gerontotoxins,

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:29.560
age-accelerating toxins,

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:31.400
such as advanced
glycation end-products,

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:34.160
also known as
glycotoxins, or AGEs,

00:33:34.160 --> 00:33:38.480
an acronym intentionally chosen
to emphasize their toxic role

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:40.040
in age-related disease.

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:42.680
AGEs occur
naturally in animals,

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:47.240
but dry cooking methods can
generate 10 to 100 times more.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:51.480
When muscle cells
rupture under dry heat,

00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:54.960
highly reactive compounds
combine with blood and body sugars

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:57.840
to form AGEs
within the meat.

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:00.080
So, even without
cutting down on meat,

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.920
you can significantly cut
down on glycotoxin intake,

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:04.960
just by switching
up cooking methods.

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:07.520
The safest ways of cooking
meat are moist methods,

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:10.178
such as boiling, poaching,
steaming, stewing.

00:34:10.178 --> 00:34:11.440
Check out what happens
when people are randomized

00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:15.240
to a meal of fried or broiled
chicken breast and veggies—

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:16.920
or the same meal with
the same ingredients—

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:18.800
including the oil—

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:22.440
but with steamed or
boiled chicken breast instead?

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:27.240
That single high AGE meal
with the fried or broiled breast

00:34:27.240 --> 00:34:34.720
induced a profound impairment
of artery function within hours.

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:37.320
The steamed chicken still
impaired artery function,

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:41.960
but not as much as
the fried or broiled.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:45.560
So, we can eat
an “age less” diet

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:48.040
by switching to moist
methods, for example,

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:53.560
but dietary approaches are said
to have “zero commercial value”

00:34:53.560 --> 00:34:57.400
and hey, stewed chicken
may not be as tasty;

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:00.160
so, why not
just take a drug

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:04.880
to block AGE absorption
every time we eat?

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:09.400
It does lower your
blood levels of AGEs.

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:13.000
It’s just activated charcoal,

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:16.120
like when people are
poisoned or for drug overdoses.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.600
I’m sure our AGEs
would also go down

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:24.440
if we chased our chicken
with a little ipecac, too.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:26.800
What about fish?

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:28.520
Unfortunately,

00:35:28.520 --> 00:35:32.480
our oceans have essentially
become humanity’s sewer.

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:35.720
Everything eventually
flows into the sea;

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:39.760
so, the consumption
of contaminated seafood

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:42.000
has become
the main route

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:47.240
of human exposure
to chemical pollutants.

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:49.720
Yes, a variety of
foods are affected,

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:53.240
but the number one
source of DDT? Fish.

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:58.040
The number one source
of PCBs? Fish.

00:35:58.040 --> 00:36:01.800
Salmon is the worst
when it comes to PCBs,

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:05.600
followed by canned tuna.

00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:09.320
Salmon also has the highest
levels of dioxin-like compounds.

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:11.320
So, on the one hand,

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.440
fish has long been viewed
as a healthy dietary component

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:16.120
because of those
long-chain-omega-3’s,

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:19.880
but on the other hand,
we’re so polluting our world

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:21.640
that we’re now
seeing heavy metals,

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:25.600
pesticides, flame retardants in fish,
forever chemicals,

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:29.640
which may explain why,
despite their omega-3’s,

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:31.000
there’s been a failure

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:34.880
to consistently observe beneﬁcial
effects of ﬁsh consumption.

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:36.200
So, if we had
a time machine

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:38.200
to go back before
the industrial revolution,

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:42.640
that would be one thing,
but now, we have studies like this,

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:46.040
where mice fed commercially
available farmed salmon ﬁllets

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:49.040
with common levels of
persistent organic pollutants

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:52.480
were found to develop
insulin resistance,

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:55.720
glucose intolerance,
abdominal obesity, fatty liver,

00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:57.000
chronic low-grade
inﬂammation.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.960
So, even just background
levels of pollutants

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:02.040
that might be
presumed to be safe

00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:05.720
could completely counteract
the potential beneﬁts of omega-3s,

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:09.800
in particular,
leading to metabolic issues.

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:14.520
For mercury,
it’s more about brain health.

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:19.200
Hundreds of thousands of babies born
with brain damage every year,

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.600
seemingly as a consequence
of their mothers

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:26.040
consuming mercury-contaminated
fish during pregnancy,

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:28.800
with the resulting
loss of intelligence

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:36.960
estimated to cause billions of dollars
of lost productivity every year.

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:40.200
In the aging literature,
there are cases like this:

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.280
a man with progressive
memory loss

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:45.760
tentatively diagnosed with
Alzheimer’s dementia.

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:51.680
His friends and family assumed
he was nearing the end of his life.

00:37:51.680 --> 00:37:55.240
But a detailed
history revealed

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:58.440
that he had consumed
swordﬁsh once or twice a week.

00:37:58.440 --> 00:38:01.480
When the fish was
removed from his diet,

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:06.400
his mercury levels fell,
and his memory bounced back.

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:08.160
No more dementia.

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:11.960
So, it seemed he didn’t
have Alzheimer’s after all,

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:16.200
but rather mercury poisoning
from a handful of monthly meals

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:19.320
of contaminated fish.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:23.240
You can see the same
remarkable reversals with hair loss.

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:24.320
Here’s a common story:

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:27.440
a perimenopausal woman
seeks help for hair loss.

00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:30.080
Blood tests indicate
elevated mercury levels.

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:31.040
And no wonder,

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:33.880
because she had
a diet high in tuna.

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:36.600
But within two months
of stopping fish,

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:39.760
her hair started
to grow back,

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:44.440
and eventually her
hair regrew completely.

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:48.160
So, doctors should consider
screening for mercury toxicity

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:49.640
when they see hair loss,

00:38:49.640 --> 00:38:52.360
since there’s something
we can do about it.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:55.000
Instructing patients to
reduce fish intake

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:56.840
could offer relief
of symptoms

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:58.200
and uncover
dietary habits

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:02.840
that may be a source of
heavy-metal-induced hair loss.

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:04.200
(Though, admittedly,

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:08.480
sometimes heavy metal
can lead to too much hair.)

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:12.560
What about alcohol?
That’s from plants!

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:16.720
Sorry to be Dr. Buzzkill,

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:21.840
but alcohol appears to be humanity’s
7th leading cause of death and disability.

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:26.440
The safest level
of drinking is none.

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:31.080
But wait a second,
what about the famous J curve,

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:34.840
where light to moderate drinkers
appear to have lower mortality rates

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:37.920
than the teetotalers
at zero drinks a day?

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:42.520
This, may just be an artifact
of the systemic misclassification

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:46.440
of former drinkers
as lifelong abstainers.

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:50.760
This leads to the so-called
“sick-quitter” effect,

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:53.440
where the reason a person
may be a nondrinker

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:55.400
is because drinking
made them sick,

00:39:55.400 --> 00:40:00.480
thereby making current drinkers
look good in comparison.

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:04.880
It’s the same reason studies
can find higher mortality

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:07.120
among those who
quit smoking

00:40:07.120 --> 00:40:09.200
compared to those
who continue to smoke.

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:11.040
It’s not that abstention
led to poor health,

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:14.920
but poor health rather
led to abstention.

00:40:14.920 --> 00:40:18.640
When researchers went back
and controlled for the error

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:24.280
of misclassifying former drinkers,
the J curve disappeared.

00:40:24.280 --> 00:40:29.200
No more apparent benefit
to moderate drinking.

00:40:29.200 --> 00:40:33.080
In other words,
compared to true abstainers,

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:36.600
the more alcohol,
the more disease,

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:42.800
with no apparent protection
at low levels of consumption.

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:45.240
Even red wine?

00:40:45.240 --> 00:40:46.040
Unfortunately,

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:49.880
interventional trials show that
wine can cause inflammation

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:53.040
within hours of consumption,
both red and white—

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:55.680
significantly more than just
like drinking sugar water,

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.000
and you get a surge of
fat in your bloodstream

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:01.600
that you don’t get drinking
“dealcoholized red wine”

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:04.600
(the same wine with
the alcohol taken out).

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:06.640
What about just
taking supplements

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:10.840
of the red wine
compound resveratrol?

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:12.080
Oh, you mean the supplement

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:17.560
found to nearly triple the loss of
brain tissue compared to placebo?

00:41:17.560 --> 00:41:22.320
No, thank you.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:25.400
The healthiest beverages
are probably water and tea.

00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:27.680
Increasing tea consumption
three cups (720 ml) a day

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:32.280
may decrease the risk of premature
death from all causes by 24 percent.

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.560
This applied to both
green and black tea,

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:37.680
though green may
have a slight advantage.

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:43.040
Part of this protection may be
a lower risk of dying of pneumonia.

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.360
There have been five
randomized, controlled trials

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:47.200
showing those
randomized to green tea

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:48.280
are about a
third less likely

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:50.680
to come down with
the flu, for example.

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:54.880
Within just one hour of drinking
a single cup (200 ml) of green tea,

00:41:54.880 --> 00:41:56.600
we can significantly cut down

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:59.400
on the rate of DNA
damage in our cells,

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:04.360
because green tea significantly boosts
the activity of a DNA repair enzyme

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:07.000
in our body that can
fix DNA damage.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:08.680
And within a month,

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:10.840
drinking two small
daily cups (400 ml) of green tea

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:15.040
can improve DNA resistance to
free radical damage in the first place,

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:19.040
indicating that green tea has
significant genoprotective effects,

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:23.200
or DNA protecting effects.

